<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-android36.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      RootNamespace is deliberately NOT SnapZap.Android.

      Inside a namespace called SnapZap.Android, the compiler resolves `Android.App` relative to
      the enclosing namespace first and looks for SnapZap.Android.App before the Android SDK's
      global Android.App. Every using of an SDK namespace then fails with an error that points at
      the using rather than at the real cause. SnapZap.Droid has no such collision.

      The *project* keeps the SnapZap.Android name because that is what it is; only the C#
      namespace differs.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>SnapZap.Droid</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      API 30 is the floor because MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE ("All files access") and the
      Settings.ActionManageAppAllFilesAccessPermission intent that requests it are API 30+
      (docs/ANDROID-PORT-PLAN.md §3). The whole storage model depends on it, so there is no
      point supporting anything older.
    -->
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>30</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>

    <ApplicationId>com.snapzap.android</ApplicationId>
    <!--
      ⚠ A second version pair, and the only one in the repo besides SnapZap.App's <Version>.
      Android requires both: ApplicationDisplayVersion is versionName (what a person reads) and
      ApplicationVersion is versionCode (a monotonic integer the platform compares to decide
      whether an APK is an upgrade — it must never go backwards).

      Keep DisplayVersion equal to SnapZap.App's <Version>. CLAUDE.md's "exactly one source" rule
      predates the Android head; it is still true for the shipping desktop artifacts, which read the
      version back out of the built .exe.

      ApplicationVersion is a build counter, not a mirror of the version name, and the two move
      independently: it must increase for any APK that lands on a device that already has an older
      one, and it must never decrease. So it is normal — and currently the case — for it to be
      ahead of what a version-name bump alone would suggest. 1.3.0 has shipped as code 2 and again
      as code 3 after the UX-review work; reverting it to 2 would leave two different APKs claiming
      the same code and make the newer one uninstallable over the older.
    -->
    <ApplicationVersion>3</ApplicationVersion>
    <ApplicationDisplayVersion>1.3.0</ApplicationDisplayVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    AdditionalProperties="IncludeAndroid=true" is required, not decorative. SnapZap.Core only
    grows its net10.0-android36.0 leg under that flag (AC-2.7's opt-in, see SnapZap.Core.csproj);
    without it this reference resolves against Core's plain net10.0 leg, which carries the macOS
    and Win32 SkiaSharp native assets and none of the Android ones — an APK that builds and then
    dies at the first decode.

    ⚠ AdditionalProperties alone is NOT sufficient, and this build fails without help. It flows
    the flag to Core's *build*, but NuGet's restore walks its own graph and does not honour it, so
    Core's obj/project.assets.json comes out with no net10.0-android36.0 target and the build dies
    with NETSDK1005 ("Assets file doesn't have a target for net10.0-android36.0"). Pass the flag
    globally instead, which reaches restore and build alike:

        dotnet build src/SnapZap.Android -p:IncludeAndroid=true

    The AdditionalProperties attribute is kept because it documents the dependency at the point it
    exists and makes the build fail loudly rather than silently resolving Core's desktop leg.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SnapZap.Core\SnapZap.Core.csproj"
                      AdditionalProperties="IncludeAndroid=true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT listed in SnapZap.slnx. Adding it makes the plain `dotnet build` at the repo
    root require an Android SDK, which is precisely the tax the opt-in decision in
    docs/ANDROID-PORT-ACS.md AC-2.7 exists to avoid for SnapZap.Core. Build it explicitly:

        dotnet build src/SnapZap.Android

    The cost is AC-2.9's, in the same shape: a project nobody builds by default can rot. That is
    an acceptable trade while this project is a stage-1 artifact rather than a shipping head; it
    stops being acceptable the moment real code lives here, at which point it needs CI coverage
    alongside `-p:IncludeAndroid=true`.
  -->

  <!--
    ⚠ There is deliberately NO ProjectReference to SnapZap.App here, and adding one does not work.

    docs/ANDROID-PORT-PLAN.md §0 bets that Android can be a fifth IAppHost: run the same
    WebApplication in-process, bound to loopback, and point a system WebView at it. That bet is
    dead, and this project is where it was proven dead. Two errors, in order:

      NETSDK1150  An Android app is a self-contained executable and cannot reference
                  SnapZap.App, which is a non-self-contained Exe. (SnapZap.App must stay
                  OutputType=Exe — exactly Exe — or Blazor's static web assets are silently
                  dropped; see the comment block in SnapZap.App.csproj.) This one has a
                  documented escape hatch, -p:ValidateExecutableReferencesMatchSelfContained=false,
                  which gets past it and straight into the real problem:

      NETSDK1082  "There was no runtime pack for Microsoft.AspNetCore.App available for the
                  specified RuntimeIdentifier 'android-arm64'."

    The second is not a configuration problem. ASP.NET Core publishes no runtime pack for any
    mobile RID — verified against nuget.org, where microsoft.aspnetcore.app.runtime.win-x64,
    .osx-arm64 and .linux-arm64 all resolve and .android-arm64 and .ios-arm64 both 404. There is
    no Kestrel on Android, so there is no in-process web server for a WebView to talk to, and the
    plan's §0 gate ("if step 2 fails outright, stop and re-scope around BlazorWebView") is met.

    This project is kept as what it actually proved: the Android toolchain produces a building,
    installable APK from this repo. That is stage 1 of the spike and it passed. Whatever replaces
    the §0 architecture builds on this, so do not delete it — but do not re-add the reference
    either, and see docs/ANDROID-PORT-ACS.md §1.6 before changing the approach.
  -->

</Project>
